FROZEN RUBRIC:
## Rubric: score the screen AND its components

Grade each dimension 0–10. Aesthetic score A = mean of hierarchy, typography/spacing, surface/color coherence. Functional score F = mean of task clarity, feedback/recovery, accessibility. Overall = 0.4A + 0.6F, rounded to one decimal. Apply the same six dimensions to each screen and each meaningful component (orb, tabs, option, input, card, button, modal). Mark genuinely inapplicable dimensions N/A and normalize; never infer an interaction score from a screenshot.

- **6 — usable but visibly rough:** task succeeds; inconsistent spacing, competing emphasis, or weak feedback is obvious. No data loss or unreachable primary action.
- **8 — clean and dependable:** one clear next action; consistent type/spacing/surface roles; responsive press/loading/error states; 44pt targets; text enlargement, contrast and reduced motion checked. Minor polish remains.
- **9 — premium and effortless:** all 8 criteria plus continuity through transitions, contextual recovery, concise humane copy, restrained delight, no distracting layout shifts; real phone and assistive-technology checks passed.
- **10 — exemplary for this task:** all 9 criteria; repeated novice use and representative accessibility/performance checks find no material friction. Reserve for demonstrated outcomes, not taste or resemblance to another app.
- **0–5:** 0 absent/unusable; 2 major path broken; 4 task possible only with workarounds; 5 works but a serious clarity/accessibility issue remains.

Hard gates: blocked or materially unanswered task/data loss/error disguised as success => overall at most 4. Essential unreadable content, inaccessible primary control or motion preference ignored => at most 5. Unverified runtime/native behavior => provisional grade, cannot certify 9/10. Averages never erase a failed gate.

Loop acceptance: every core screen and critical component >=8, A and F each >=8, no hard-gate failure. Record before/after screenshot at 390×844, action and result, normal/200% text, reduced motion, empty/loading/error/selected state and any untested boundary. Change one issue at a time; keep every failed observation. Browser checks do not prove native haptics, voice, keyboard or screen reader behavior.



Authorized loop threshold is A >=9 and F >=9 for every screen and meaningful component from both independent graders. This supersedes the report's >=8 acceptance sentence; its evidence requirements and hard gates remain unchanged. Native and physical assistive-technology coverage is unverified, so no 9/10 certification is permitted on that evidence. Grade honestly; the target is not a requested score.


Stage clarification from firstmate inbox004: continue scoring the browser implementation provisionally until every screen and component reaches A>=9 and F>=9 there; firstmate will then arrange the physical iPhone/VoiceOver pass. For this stage, judge the demonstrated browser behavior across the full numeric range and record missing physical/native proof separately, rather than lowering every browser score solely for that deferred boundary. This does NOT certify a full/native9/10 or waive a browser accessibility defect. Keep all other anchors, dimensions and failure gates unchanged. Never award a target score merely to complete the loop.

Baseline current production has127tests,294layout states across360/390/430 normal200%,1740targets,489contrast checks,16entrances,actual local-model and60-second recovery pass. Seven opening modes and isolated near60fps/max16.8ms motion pass. Physical/native remains deferred. These facts support baseline only.
All captures390px wide,844px tall, plus500px custom-answer viewport. Selected static Fraunces/Manrope and Open mist stay fixed. Baselines are the real app with controlled layout records. The same question and custom answer are used across variants. Check-in record is fictional, with the same original question, headline and not-a-guarantee qualifier throughout; it is not model-quality evidence. Prototype controls illustrate routes; no actual validation, age decision, persistence, calculation, model or social operation occurs. No credit for missing draft retention, focus, semantics, keyboard/native or recovery. Prototype checks only establish font loading, page errors and horizontal document overflow.
QUESTION: baseline shows utility orb, fixed progress/back header, large serif question and answer rows that acknowledge for180ms then advance (immediate with reduced motion). A removes the utility orb, uses inline progress and smaller serif task role with direct answer rows. B uses explicit radio selection and Continue, allowing inspection/change before committing; extra tap. C keeps a small orb/progress medallion, centered question and direct answers. 'In my own words' opens custom answer; prototypes do not infer/send a plan. Selected B capture is supplied.
CUSTOM: baseline uses fixed progress and Back to choices in one header, question, labeled field and Continue. A places progress, separate back-to-choices row, question, input and action in one scrolling sequence. B adds a review step showing the draft before Use this answer or Edit; extra step and retention unimplemented. C uses small orb/progress medallion and body-sized question context rather than a large serif question. The populated answer is identical across all;500px is synthetic, not a physical keyboard. Review captures supplied for B.
BIRTHDAY: baseline has optional birthday with three labeled fields, one row at normal and stacked at200%, Continue and Skip below. A uses a compact day/month/year grid, two rows at200%, Skip adjacent to optionality. B uses one explicitly formatted YYYY-MM-DD text field and example; compact but adds format burden. C uses day, month and year steps with previous-part and Skip; additional navigation and required draft preservation. All variants intend the existing age13+ confirmation after Skip, under13 restriction and correction rules, but none implements them. Month/year step captures supplied for C. Do not credit valid-date or age enforcement from the sketches.
CHECK-IN: baseline shows original question, optional saved forecast, What happened, immediate persisted/correctable outcomes. A uses a compact task heading and original question, optional forecast before direct outcomes. B presents radio outcomes and explicit Save before the optional full forecast. C requires reviewing the forecast first, then Record an outcome leading to the choices; a Review forecast again route returns. Context content remains complete and no reminder date changes are proposed. Expanded forecast/outcome-step captures supplied. Actual saving, corrected outcomes and focus are not implemented in prototypes.
Judge hierarchy, type/spacing, surfaces, clarity, control discovery and tradeoffs in these demonstrated views. Do not assume extra steps are better, or that fewer elements automatically improve clarity. Read only supplied images and docs/ux-loop/expansion-38/checks.json if necessary. Return concrete findings and honest limitations.

